From: godotjs
Subject: [godotjs] libffi: put win64_armasm.obj where the binaries that need it link it

Windows/arm64 builds libffi's assembly by assembling src/aarch64/win64_armasm.S into
a standalone object and naming that object in the libffi target's own `libraries`.
libffi is a static library, so nothing ever links it and the entry is never used: the
three symbols the object exports - ffi_call_SYSV, ffi_closure_SYSV, ffi_closure_SYSV_V,
all called from src/aarch64/ffi.c - stay undefined, and the first binary to link
libffi.lib stops at its link step (node_mksnapshot.exe: error LNK2019 three times,
then LNK1120). Every other platform gets those symbols inside the archive, because
there sysv.S is one of libffi's sources.

Move the object to the generated-sources directory and repeat the entry under
link_settings, the block gyp merges into the link line of every dependent - the same
route deps/crates/crates.gyp already uses to reach those binaries.

The directory matters. A path handed over through link_settings is resolved in the
depending target's own directories, and INTERMEDIATE_DIR differs per target: keeping
it there asked link.exe for obj\node_mksnapshot.gen\win64_armasm.obj, which is not
where the action writes. SHARED_INTERMEDIATE_DIR is the one directory all targets of a
toolset agree on, and host and target toolsets each have their own - which is right
here, because a cross build's host tool links the host libffi. Windows/arm64 only,
and the object is still named where it was, so nothing that saw it before stops
seeing it.
diff --git a/deps/libffi/libffi.gyp b/deps/libffi/libffi.gyp
index f3122a4..8842547 100644
--- a/deps/libffi/libffi.gyp
+++ b/deps/libffi/libffi.gyp
@@ -311,8 +311,25 @@
         ['OS == "win" and target_arch == "arm64"', {
           # Link the prebuilt object file directly
           'libraries': [
-            '<(INTERMEDIATE_DIR)/win64_armasm.obj',
+            '<(SHARED_INTERMEDIATE_DIR)/libffi/win64_armasm.obj',
           ],
+          # The block above only reaches a link of libffi itself, and a static library
+          # is never linked, so dependents stopped with unresolved ffi_call_SYSV /
+          # ffi_closure_SYSV / ffi_closure_SYSV_V - the three entry points this object
+          # exports. link_settings is the block gyp merges into the link line of every
+          # dependent.
+          #
+          # The path goes through SHARED_INTERMEDIATE_DIR rather than staying in
+          # INTERMEDIATE_DIR for a reason: a linked-in path from link_settings is
+          # resolved in the depending target's own directories, and INTERMEDIATE_DIR
+          # differs from target to target - naming it there asked for
+          # obj/node_mksnapshot.gen/win64_armasm.obj, a file that never exists. The
+          # generated-sources directory is the one place every target agrees on.
+          'link_settings': {
+            'libraries': [
+              '<(SHARED_INTERMEDIATE_DIR)/libffi/win64_armasm.obj',
+            ],
+          },
           'actions': [
             {
               'action_name': 'assemble_win64_arm_asm',
@@ -326,7 +343,7 @@
                 '<(SHARED_INTERMEDIATE_DIR)/libffi/fficonfig.h',
               ],
               'outputs': [
-                '<(INTERMEDIATE_DIR)/win64_armasm.obj',
+                '<(SHARED_INTERMEDIATE_DIR)/libffi/win64_armasm.obj',
               ],
               'action': [
                 '<(python)',
